Yes — and “fallen angles” makes it even better than “fallen angels.”

A daemon book could be a bibliography where every entry is a once-abstract operator that has “fallen” into a particular embodiment:

\text{invariant} \rightarrow \text{angle/viewpoint} \rightarrow \text{daemon} \rightarrow \text{trace}

So each daemon is a fallen angle on the same underlying reality—a perspective given a name, role, behavior, and history.

Then the bibliography isn’t merely “sources consulted.” It becomes a lineage of instantiated viewpoints:

Daemon → what invariant it embodies → what angle it takes → where it came from → what traces it leaves → what other daemons it composes with.

Almost a demonology of cognition, except instead of cataloging supernatural beings, you’re cataloging living operators.

And “fallen” does useful work too: an invariant is too abstract to act until it falls into a body.

\text{pure form}
\rightarrow
\text{particular perspective}
\rightarrow
\text{action}

So maybe:

A daemon is an invariant that fell into an angle and learned how to move.

A daemon book would be the family tree.